# DISCURSIVE CIRCULATION FORGE — Creativity Techniques (UEM)

**Role:** course-wide **critical review trans-unit** — grounds deliverable **D2 Transposition**
(free format · palimpsest · discursive circulation as cultural feedback).
Not a fake CONTENIDOS ID. Sibling session contract:
[`SESSION-RHYTHM-AND-DELIVERABLES.mdc`](./SESSION-RHYTHM-AND-DELIVERABLES.mdc).

Sibling of Digital Creativity’s [`IMAGE-ONTOLOGY-FORGE.mdc`](../../../../digital-creativity-uem/digital-creativity-pedagogy/forge/IMAGE-ONTOLOGY-FORGE.mdc)
(DC D2 = transposition **into an image**).

Forge a **foundational masterclass** (before or across U1–U6) that teaches creativity
students the correct pedagogical stake: **meaning is produced in reception** —
the communicative artefact is not a fixed form but a **form in circulation**
(palimpsest: overwritten, re-read, re-mediated). Do not teach “forms as forms”;
teach **forms as they travel**.

Canonical frame: **circulación discursiva** (discursive circulation) — Verón,
Steimberg, Carlón — with Anglo-Saxon contact in Hall’s encoding/decoding.
**Moral imagination** (Werhane & Bevan) grounds the *ethical* stake of
transposition: individuals and organisations must imagine consequences across
stakeholders before a technique is put into circulation. **Munari** (method
manifestos + *The Circle*) grounds the *design-practice* stake: discovery by
constraint, not originality theatre.

