## Pedagogical thesis (binding)

```text
Meaning is not sealed at production.
Meaning happens in the gap between production and recognition.
Creativity techniques put signs into circulation; audiences re-signify them.
The studio artefact is a communicative object in motion — a palimpsest under brief.
```

Students must leave able to name **where** a technique acts (language / medium /
support) and **what happens** when the piece is put into discursive circulation.

## Core triad — do not collapse these terms

| Spanish | English (course) | What it is | Examples |
| ------- | ---------------- | ---------- | -------- |
| **Lenguaje** | Language / code / semiotic system | Structured system of signs, rules, symbols that *generate* meaning | Visual code, typographic system, brand lexicon, musical language |
| **Medio** | Medium (pl. media) | Socio-technical *channel*, form, or institution through which messages are organised | Television, cinema, print press, Instagram, pitch deck as institutional genre |
| **Soporte** | Support / carrier / material substrate | Concrete physical or technical material that *holds* signifiers | Paper, canvas, magnetic tape, screen, optical disc, PDF file |

Teaching rule: a creativity technique always lands somewhere in this triad.
Example — *Oblique Strategies* cards are a **language** (constraint prompts) on a
**support** (cards/paper/app) circulating in a **medium** (studio workshop /
band session). Never call the screen “the medium” when you mean the substrate.

## Discursive circulation — required vocabulary

| Term (ES) | Term (EN) | Role in studio |
| --------- | --------- | -------------- |
| Circulación discursiva | Discursive circulation | Non-linear gap between production and recognition where social meaning is negotiated |
| Puesta en circulación discursiva | Enactment of discursive circulation | Inserting a text/sign system into the social sphere so it can be interpreted and transformed |
| Condiciones de producción | Conditions of production | Brief, institution, technical supports, semiotic rules that generate the text |
| Condiciones de reconocimiento | Conditions of recognition / reception | Cultural and semiotic rules under which publics decode or re-signify |
| Desajuste / décalage | Gap / asymmetry | Production never guarantees recognition (Verón) |
| Contrato de lectura | Reading / enunciative contract | Implicit pact of tone, style, trust between enunciator and enunciatee |
| Mediatización | Mediatization | Historical process by which practices become structured by media systems |
| Hipermediatización | Hypermediatization (Carlón) | Bidirectional flow mass media ↔ social platforms; linear circulation breaks |

Theoretical comparison to keep short in slides:

| Focus | English | Primary theorists |
| ----- | ------- | ----------------- |
| Circulación | Discursive circulation | Eliseo Verón |
| Géneros / estilos | Generic transgression & recirculation | Oscar Steimberg |
| Hipercirculación | Hypercirculation / inter-systemic | Mario Carlón |

Anglo parallel (one slide max): Hall (1980) encoding/decoding — not a substitute
for Verón, a contact point for bilingual cohorts.

## Ontology for creativity techniques (detection axes)

Every student artefact is classified on **three** independent axes (do not merge):

### A. Triad location

```text
language_code
medium_institution
support_substrate
hybrid_triad
```

### B. Circulation moment

```text
conditions_of_production
puesta_en_circulacion
conditions_of_recognition
gap_desajuste
hypercirculation_loop
```

### C. Technique job (course CONTENIDOS)

```text
generate
select
develop
workplace_transfer
tech_mediation
personal_agency
```

Students must never infer “the message got through” from polish alone. Record
brief, support, medium, intended reading contract, and one plausible misreading.

## Required lecture arc

1. Why forms-as-forms fail creativity education — reception generates meaning.
2. Lenguaje / medio / soporte — three terms, three mistakes to avoid.
3. Putting a technique into circulation (puesta en circulación).
4. Production vs recognition; the gap (desajuste).
5. Reading contract in pitches, ads, and studio critiques.
6. Palimpsest: iteration as overwriting under the same brief.
7. Hypermediatization: platform loops and originality theatre (tie U5 lightly).
8. Detection exercise: classify five artefacts on axes A–C.

## 120-minute session

```text
00–25  students map one familiar ad/post: language / medium / support
25–55  lecture: triad + discursive circulation + gap + reading contract
55–85  circulation cards: production conditions → puesta → recognition → gap
85–120 workshop: rewrite one card after peer recognition; save to process portfolio
```

Student slideshow: **≤ 6 main ideas** after title (same law as
`STUDENT-SLIDESHOW-FORGE.mdc`). Professor brief holds the full theory ladder.

